  "body": {
    "id": "prop:fully-interacted-corollary",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "Along the retained even tail \\(n\\ge N_0\\), the fully interacted estimator satisfies \\(\\sqrt n(\\widehat\\tau_{\\mathrm{Lin},n}-\\tau_n)\\Rightarrow\\mathcal N(0,s^2)\\). Its residual-Neyman variance based on \\(\\widehat u_n\\) yields an asymptotically conservative Wald interval; coverage is asymptotically exact when \\(u_n-s_n^2\\to0\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "tailIndex",
      "sampleSize",
      "linEstimator",
      "ate",
      "limitResidualScale",
      "estimatedEnvelope",
      "envelopeVariance",
      "residualScale"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:regular-tail",
      "thm:radial-entrance-law-design-class",
      "lem:stopped-moment-consistency",
      "def:lin-estimator",
      "def:variance-envelope",
      "def:ate-estimator",
      "def:score-system",
      "lem:initial-score-clt",
      "lem:regular-tail-wellposedness"
    ],
    "justification": "Full interactions remove the clipped covariate component only after terminal slope consistency and limiting residual independence have been established under the stopped design.",
    "gap": "Lin proves this Gaussian adjustment under complete randomization, and Li--Ding and Zhao--Ding prove related cancellation under uniform constrained designs, not path-selected PSRR.",
    "consumer": "Applied analysts can use standard fully interacted software and robust residual variance after an original PSRR allocation.",
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(\\widehat\\beta_{z,n}\\) be the within-arm OLS slope.  Because the population covariates are centered and the arms are equal, \\(\\bar x_{1,n}+\\bar x_{0,n}=0\\).  The coefficient on treatment in the fully interacted regression is the difference of the two fitted intercepts, so the identity\n\\[\n \\widehat\\tau_{\\mathrm{Lin},n}\n =\\widehat\\tau_n-\\frac12(\\widehat\\beta_{1,n}+\\widehat\\beta_{0,n})^{\\mathsf T}\n (\\bar x_{1,n}-\\bar x_{0,n}) \\tag{29}\n\\]\nholds whenever the arm Gram matrices are nonsingular; the Moore--Penrose version agrees asymptotically because singularity has probability tending to zero.  Stopped-moment consistency gives \\(\\widehat\\beta_{z,n}-\\beta_{z,n}=o_{\\Pr}(1)\\).  Moreover, directly from the score definition,\n\\[\n \\bar x_{1,n}-\\bar x_{0,n}=\\frac2{\\sqrt n}S_{xx,n}^{1/2}D_n(W_{n,\\mathrm{stop}}),\n \\qquad S_{xx,n}^{1/2}(\\beta_{1,n}+\\beta_{0,n})=b_n. \\tag{30}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\|D_n(W_{n,\\mathrm{stop}})\\|\\le\\sqrt a\\), (29)--(30) and the exact ATE decomposition imply\n\\[\n \\sqrt n(\\widehat\\tau_{\\mathrm{Lin},n}-\\tau_n)\n =b_n^{\\mathsf T}D_n+s_nE_n-b_n^{\\mathsf T}D_n+o_{\\Pr}(1)\n =s_nE_n+o_{\\Pr}(1). \\tag{31}\n\\]\nThe radial-entrance theorem and \\(s_n\\to s>0\\) therefore give the asserted \\(\\mathcal N(0,s^2)\\) limit.\n\nLet \\(z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\) be the standard-normal two-sided critical value.  The residual-Neyman interval has half-width \\(z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\sqrt{\\widehat u_n/n}\\).  By stopped-moment consistency, \\(\\widehat u_n\\to_{\\Pr}u\\), while the variance identity gives \\(u\\ge s^2\\).  From (31),\n\\[\n \\liminf_n\\Pr\\left\\{\\left|\\sqrt n(\\widehat\\tau_{\\mathrm{Lin},n}-\\tau_n)\\right|\n \\le z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\sqrt{\\widehat u_n}\\right\\}\n \\ge1-\\alpha.\n\\]\nIf \\(u_n-s_n^2\\to0\\), then \\(u=s^2\\), and the limiting standardized statistic is exactly standard normal; continuity of the Gaussian distribution makes the limiting coverage exactly \\(1-\\alpha\\).",
